   "text": "Best Power Stations Under $300: 3 Honest Picks | Volt & Valley \n Volt & Valley is reader-funded. We earn a commission when you buy through our links, at no extra cost to you, as an Amazon Associate. Commission never decides a ranking — see how we choose and our affiliate disclosure .\n At a glance\n Quick picks\n Tap any row to jump to the full write-up. Weight is a column here because it is the spec that decides whether you actually carry the thing.\n # Product Best for Capacity · Weight Price \n 1 BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 600W continuous and a 1,500W surge out of 288Wh — double the output of every other unit in this capacity class. Best under $300 overall 288Wh\n9.5 lb $279.99 · View on Amazon → \n 2 EcoFlow RIVER 3 Plus The successor to the RIVER 2 Pro: the only sub-11 lb unit here that still expands past 800Wh. Best if you might need more later 286Wh\n10.4 lb $299.00 · View on Amazon → \n 3 Jackery Explorer 300 The lightest unit here with real AC outlets, and honest about its ceiling: phones, lights, a laptop, a CPAP. Not a fridge unit, and not the long-life chemistry the newer small units use. Lightest real AC outlet 293Wh\n7.1 lb $289.00 · View on Amazon → \n #ad Prices are read from Amazon’s API and refreshed daily; the date each one was read is shown beside the buy buttons below, and a price that stops refreshing disappears rather than going stale. Weights and capacities are manufacturer-published figures; sources are listed at the foot of this page.\n Our pick\n BLUETTI Elite 30 V2\n 600W continuous and a 1,500W lifting figure out of 288Wh at 9.5 lb, with a 10 ms switchover — double the output of most units at this budget.\n 288Wh · 600W continuous · 9.5 lb · 200W solar in\n $279.99 · View on Amazon $449.00 ( 38 % off) → #ad We earn a commission at no extra cost to you. How this is funded . Price as of October 30, 2026 .\n What $300 actually buys, stated plainly\n Under $300 is a single tier, and it is roughly 300 watt-hours of battery. Not 500, not 768. Every unit worth buying at this budget sits between 286Wh and 293Wh, which is close enough to identical that capacity is not a deciding factor — the contest is entirely about inverter output, weight, cycle life and how fast the thing refills. When we checked this band on 2026-10-19, the next capacity step up, a 512Wh unit, sat meaningfully above $300, and a 768Wh unit further above that again. The cliff is real and it is steep. \n So the honest framing of this page is: 300Wh is a genuinely useful amount of electricity for a particular set of jobs, and completely inadequate for a different set. It will keep phones, lights, a laptop, a router, a camera kit and a CPAP going for a night or two. It will not keep a refrigerator cold through an outage, run a space heater, or power a kettle more than a couple of times. If your actual requirement is the second list, the right move is to spend more rather than to buy at this budget and be disappointed — best budget power stations under $500 is the next band up and it buys about three times the battery. \n How we ranked these\n Four criteria, applied in this order, and every figure is the manufacturer's own published number read from the pages listed at the foot of this page. We have not bench-tested these units and we do not claim to have — how we choose sets out exactly what our method can and cannot tell you. \n Continuous AC output. At this capacity every unit holds roughly the same battery, so output is what separates them, and the spread is 2x — 300W at one end and 600W at the other. Output decides *what* plugs in at all; capacity only decides how long it runs. \n Cycle life and chemistry. The difference between 500 cycles and 3,000 cycles is the difference between a unit that fades inside two years of regular use and one that outlasts the car you keep it in. At a low budget this is the specification most likely to cost you money twice. \n Weight. This is the tier people actually carry, so the pounds matter more here than anywhere else on the site. The spread across these three is 3.3 lb on a 7 lb base — proportionally enormous. \n Recharge speed and solar ceiling. A small battery you can refill in an hour behaves like a bigger one. The published solar ceiling also matters more here than it looks, because a 200W input into a 290Wh battery is a genuinely fast solar refill, unlike the same figure on a large unit. \n The three units, side by side\n The sub-$300 class on published manufacturer figures. Watt-hours per pound is our arithmetic. Blank cells are figures the manufacturer does not publish. BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 EcoFlow RIVER 3 Plus Jackery Explorer 300 \n Capacity 288Wh 286Wh 293Wh \n AC continuous 600W 600W 300W \n Surge / lifting 1,500W (resistive) 1,200W 500W \n Weight 9.5 lb 10.4 lb 7.1 lb \n Wh per pound 30.3 27.5 41.3 \n Chemistry LiFePO4 LiFePO4 Not published \n Cycle rating 3,000+ 3,000 to 80% 500 to 80%+ \n Max solar input 200W 220W Not published \n Full AC recharge 70 min 60 min About 2.5 hr \n UPS switchover 10 ms or less Not published Not published \n Expandable No To 858Wh No \n Warranty 5 years Not published 2 years \n Two rows decide this page. The AC continuous row splits the group in half — 600W or 300W, with nothing in between — and 600W is the difference between a unit that runs your television and one that does not. The cycle rating row splits it the same way: 3,000 against 500. The Jackery wins the weight column outright and wins it by enough to matter if you walk with the thing, but it loses both of the rows that decide how much use you get out of the purchase. \n What 300 watt-hours runs\n The figures below are nameplate bands . Read the plate on your own equipment, because the spread inside a product category is wider than the spread between these three units. Runtimes allow for inverter conversion losses, so they assume you never get the full rated capacity out to an AC socket. \n Runtime from about 290Wh of usable battery, by load. Load Typical draw Runtime \n Phone charge, full 15-20Wh each 12-15 charges \n Router and modem 10-20W 14-24 hours \n CPAP, humidifier off 30-60W About 1 night \n Laptop, working 50-65W 4-5 hours \n Camera batteries 10-16Wh each 15-20 batteries \n String lights 10-20W 15-25 hours \n Starlink Mini 20-40W continuous 7-12 hours \n 12V camping fridge 45W cycling at 40% 12-14 hours \n Television, 50 in 80-120W 2-3 hours \n Full-size refrigerator 150W cycling at 35% 4-5 hours \n Electric kettle 1,500W nameplate Will not run on any unit here \n Read that table top to bottom and the shape of this tier is obvious: 300Wh is days of small DC loads, one night of a CPAP, an evening of a television, and a few hours of anything with a compressor. The kettle row is the honest bottom line — no 300Wh unit will boil water in any meaningful way, whatever the surge figure says, because even if the inverter copes the battery is gone in minutes. Can a power station run a refrigerator and what size power station do I need both work through this arithmetic with your own numbers. \n The near-misses, named\n Two units that sit just outside this page\n The Anker SOLIX C300 is the obvious fourth name in this class — 288Wh, 300W, 9.1 lb, a 10 ms switchover and 25 dB of quoted noise, which is the quietest figure anywhere in our registry. It is not ranked here because we could not place it in this band with confidence: the last price we recorded for it sat just above $300, and on 2026-10-19 its Amazon listing returned no price at all. It is an easy recommendation whenever it is in stock under the line — check the button on its own entries. Anker also sells a SOLIX C300 DC , which is the cheapest way into this category by a clear margin — but it is a USB-only unit with no AC outlet at all, and Anker publishes no weight, chemistry, cycle life or warranty for it. We will not build a product card on four blank fields. If every device you own charges over USB-C, it is worth a look on Anker's own page. \n We also checked two units that Amazon still stocks and left both out. The plain EcoFlow RIVER 2 at 256Wh sits comfortably inside this budget, but EcoFlow no longer publishes a product page for it in the US, so there is no manufacturer specification to quote. The same applies to the older EcoFlow DELTA 2 . Featuring a unit whose maker has stopped documenting it means publishing numbers from a retailer listing, and that is not a standard this site keeps. \n How to choose between the three\n Will you carry it on foot? If yes, the Jackery's 7.1 lb settles it and the cycle-life compromise is the price. If it rides in a car, weight is almost irrelevant at this scale and the other two are better units. \n Will you plug in anything over 300W? A television, a games console, a blender, a coffee maker, a desktop computer. If yes, the Jackery is out and you are choosing between the BLUETTI and the EcoFlow. \n Might 300Wh stop being enough? If there is any chance of that, the EcoFlow's path to 858Wh is the only upgrade route on this page. Everything else here is permanent at 290Wh. \n Do you want it to carry a router through a cut? Only BLUETTI publishes a switchover time in this group, at 10 ms or less. The others may well do it; they do not publish it, and we will not guess on their behalf. Power station as a UPS for wifi and router covers whether that job is worth a power station at all. \n Is solar part of the plan? 200W into 288Wh, or 220W into 286Wh, is a fast refill — roughly two good hours. That is the one place where this tier genuinely outperforms the big units, which take all day. Best portable solar panels for power stations matches panels to these input ceilings. \n When to spend more instead\n Three jobs sit outside this budget entirely, and buying here for any of them ends in a second purchase. Keeping a refrigerator cold through an outage needs about a kilowatt-hour a day, so 300Wh is a few hours — best portable power stations for home backup starts where this page stops. Anything that makes heat — a kettle, a toaster, a space heater, a hair dryer — is out of reach on both output and capacity. And a long trip with a fridge running needs the 700Wh-and-up class: our EcoFlow RIVER 2 Pro review covers the unit that holds 768Wh and still weighs under 20 lb, which is the natural next step from this page. \n On price\n This site shows the live Amazon price inside each buy button rather than printing a number in the copy, because a figure typed into a page is wrong within a week. The three units above were all selling under $300 when this page was written on 2026-10-19; check the button for today's number before you decide. Nothing in the ranking above rests on a price claim — the order is set by published specifications and fit. \n The picks in full\n 1 . Best under $300 overall\n BLUETTI Elite 30 V2\n Capacity 288Wh \n Chemistry LiFePO4 (automotive-grade) \n AC output 600W continuous / 1,500W surge \n Weight 9.5 lb \n Max solar input 200W \n Cycle rating Over 3,000 cycles; 10-year lifespan \n AC recharge Full in 70 min \n Warranty 5 years \n $279.99 · View on Amazon $449.00 ( 38 % off) → #ad We earn a commission at no extra cost to you. How this is funded . Price as of October 30, 2026 .\n Twice the inverter of most units at this budget, in the same 288Wh box. BLUETTI publishes 600W continuous with a 1,500W Power Lifting figure, against the 300W and 500W that the traditional small-unit specification gives you. That difference is not academic: 600W covers a television, a desktop computer, a blender, a slow cooker and most coffee makers, and 300W covers none of them. At this budget it is the single biggest capability gap between units, and it costs nothing in weight — 9.5 lb is within half a pound of the smallest unit here. \n The rest of the sheet is current-generation throughout. Automotive-grade LiFePO4 cells, over 3,000 cycles and a ten-year stated lifespan, a full recharge in 70 minutes, 200W of solar input, a UPS switchover of 10 ms or less, nine output ports including USB-C PD at up to 140W, and a five-year warranty. The 140W USB-C port matters more than it looks: it charges a modern laptop directly on DC without waking the inverter, which skips the conversion loss and keeps the fan off. \n The honest limits. BLUETTI states plainly that this model does not support battery expansion, so 288Wh is permanent. The 1,500W Power Lifting figure is a lifting mode for resistive loads rather than a surge rating for motors — do not read it as headroom for a compressor. And BLUETTI publishes no dimensions, no full port breakdown and no retention threshold on its cycle claim, so three fields in our spec table stay blank rather than get filled from a retailer listing. \n Reasons to buy\n + 600W continuous — double the usual output at this budget \n + 1,500W Power Lifting for resistive loads \n + 10 ms or less UPS switchover \n + Full recharge in 70 minutes; 200W solar input \n + LiFePO4, 3,000+ cycles, 5-year warranty \n Reasons to avoid\n − No battery expansion — BLUETTI states the model does not support it \n − Power Lifting is a resistive-load figure, not a motor surge rating \n − No published dimensions or full port breakdown \n − Cycle claim carries no published retention threshold \n Buy it if: you want the widest appliance envelope this budget can buy. \n Skip it if: you expect to grow the battery later. \n A note on these figures: BLUETTI publishes 288Wh, 600W continuous, a 1,500W lifting/surge figure, 200W maximum solar input, a 70-minute full AC recharge, a UPS switchover of 10 ms or less, 'over 3,000 cycles and a 10-year lifespan', a 5-year warranty, nine output ports and 4.3 kg. The 9.5 lb figure is our conversion of BLUETTI's 4.3 kg. BLUETTI does not publish dimensions, a full port breakdown or a cycle-life retention threshold on its product page, so those are left blank rather than taken from a retailer listing.\n 2 . Best if you might need more later\n EcoFlow RIVER 3 Plus\n Capacity 286Wh \n Chemistry LiFePO4 \n AC output 600W continuous / 1,200W surge \n Weight 10.4 lb \n Max solar input 220W \n Cycle rating 3,000 cycles to 80% capacity \n AC recharge 0-100% in 60 min \n Warranty Not published on the US product page \n $299.00 · View on Amazon → #ad We earn a commission at no extra cost to you. How this is funded . Price as of October 30, 2026 .\n The only unit at this budget that can be grown. EcoFlow publishes an expansion battery that takes the RIVER 3 Plus from 286Wh to 858Wh — a tripling, with no cable between the two units. That is the cheap insurance against sizing this purchase wrong, and at a budget where a mistake means buying a whole second power station, it is worth real money. Everything else on the sheet is competitive rather than outstanding: 600W continuous with a 1,200W surge, 10.4 lb, 220W of solar input and a full recharge in 60 minutes, the fastest here. \n It is also the best-documented small unit in this group for ports. Three AC outlets, a 100W USB-C and two 12W USB-A ports, in a 9.2 x 9.1 x 5.8 inch footprint that is the only published dimension set among these three. If the unit has to fit a specific gap — a cabinet, a trunk well, a shelf above a desk — this is the one you can measure before you buy. LiFePO4 cells at 3,000 cycles to 80% capacity match the class. \n Two things to know. EcoFlow does not publish a warranty term for this model on its US product page, which is unusual and is the one field we would most like filled in — check the term on the listing you buy from. And it does not publish a UPS switchover time either, so if you want this unit to carry a router through a cut, you are buying an unstated specification. The expansion battery is also a second purchase at close to the price of the unit itself; the capability is real, the upgrade is not cheap. \n Reasons to buy\n + Expands from 286Wh to 858Wh with a wire-free extra battery \n + 600W continuous, 1,200W surge \n + Fastest full recharge here at 60 minutes \n + 220W solar input — the highest in this group \n + Published dimensions: 9.2 x 9.1 x 5.8 in \n Reasons to avoid\n − No warranty term published on EcoFlow's US product page \n − No published UPS switchover time \n − The expansion battery costs close to the unit itself \n − 10.4 lb — the heaviest of these three \n Buy it if: you suspect 300Wh may not be enough in a year. \n Skip it if: you want a stated warranty and switchover before you buy. \n Read the full EcoFlow RIVER 3 Plus review → \n 3 . Lightest real AC outlet\n Jackery Explorer 300\n Capacity 293Wh \n Chemistry Lithium-ion (Jackery does not publish the cell type) \n AC output 300W continuous / 500W surge \n Weight 7.1 lb \n Max solar input Not published \n Cycle rating 500 cycles to 80%+ capacity \n AC recharge About 2.5 hr full, using the 90W wall charger and a 60W USB-C PD charger together \n Warranty 2 years \n $289.00 · View on Amazon → #ad We earn a commission at no extra cost to you. How this is funded . Price as of October 30, 2026 .\n 7.1 lb with two proper AC sockets is the lightest genuine mains power you can carry at this budget, and for a specific kind of buyer that is the only specification that matters. A photographer charging batteries at a trailhead, a camper who walks in from the car, somebody who wants a unit that lives in a daypack rather than a trunk — this is two and a half pounds lighter than the BLUETTI and 3.3 pounds lighter than the EcoFlow, which is a real difference when you are carrying it rather than driving it. \n 293Wh, 300W continuous with a 500W peak, and a 60W USB-C port that works in both directions. Jackery publishes a full recharge of about 2.5 hours using the 90W wall charger alongside a 60W USB-C PD charger. Within its envelope it does the job well: phones, cameras, lights, a laptop, a CPAP without its heated humidifier, a small fan. \n This is also the unit with the most caveats here, and they are worth stating plainly. Jackery does not publish the cell chemistry for the Explorer 300 , and it rates the unit at 500 cycles to 80%+ capacity — one sixth of the 3,000-cycle ratings the LiFePO4 units here carry. The warranty is two years rather than five. 300W of output rules out the television, the coffee maker and the blender the other two will run. And Jackery publishes no maximum solar input figure and no dimensions for this model. Buy it for the weight, knowing you are buying an older design. \n Reasons to buy\n + 7.1 lb — the lightest unit here with real AC sockets \n + Two AC outlets at 300W, 500W peak \n + 60W USB-C that charges the unit and your laptop \n + Simple, proven and the longest-selling design in this group \n Reasons to avoid\n − 500 cycles to 80% — one sixth of the LiFePO4 units here \n − Jackery does not publish the cell chemistry \n − 2-year warranty against 5 years elsewhere \n − 300W output rules out televisions, blenders and coffee makers \n − No published solar input ceiling or dimensions \n Buy it if: the unit gets carried on foot and the loads are small. \n Skip it if: you will charge it often enough for cycle life to matter. \n Read the full Jackery Explorer 300 review → \n A note on these figures: Jackery publishes 293Wh, 300W output with a 500W peak, 7.1 lb, 500 cycles to 80%+ capacity, a 2-year standard warranty and a ~2.5 hr recharge using the 90W wall charger alongside a 60W USB-C PD charger. It does not publish a cell chemistry, a maximum solar input figure or dimensions for this model, so all three are left blank here rather than taken from a retailer listing. Jackery's newer Explorer 300 v2 and 300 Plus (288Wh, LiFePO4) are sold on jackery.com but have no Amazon listing, so this page covers the Explorer 300, which is the model actually purchasable through our link.\n Questions\n Frequently asked questions\n What is the best power station under $300? + The BLUETTI Elite 30 V2, on the published specifications. It gives you 600W of continuous AC against the 300W that a traditional small unit offers, out of the same 288Wh and at 9.5 lb, with LiFePO4 cells, a 10 ms UPS switchover, a 70-minute full recharge and a five-year warranty. The EcoFlow RIVER 3 Plus is the pick if you might need to expand later; the Jackery Explorer 300 is the pick if you carry the unit on foot. \n How many watt-hours do you get for under $300? + About 300Wh. Every unit genuinely worth buying at this budget sits between 286Wh and 293Wh, and the next capacity step up — around 500Wh — sold above $300 when we checked on 2026-10-19. Capacity is effectively fixed in this band, so choose on output, cycle life and weight instead. \n Will a power station under $300 run a refrigerator? + A 12V camping fridge, for about twelve to fourteen hours. A full-size household refrigerator will start and run on a 600W unit but 290Wh only covers four to five hours of it, so it is not an outage solution. If a fridge is the reason you are buying, you need at least the 1 kWh class. \n Is a 300W or a 600W power station better at this price? + 600W, if the price is the same — and at this budget it often is. 600W runs a television, a desktop computer, a blender, a slow cooker and most coffee makers; 300W runs none of those. The only reason to take 300W is weight, and that is a genuine reason: the 300W unit here is 2.4 lb lighter. \n Does cycle life matter on a cheap power station? + More than anywhere else, because it decides whether you buy again. The LiFePO4 units here are rated at 3,000-plus cycles; the Jackery Explorer 300 is rated at 500 cycles to 80%+ capacity. If you charge the unit weekly, 500 cycles is roughly a decade and fine. If you charge it daily, it is under two years, and the cheaper unit becomes the expensive one. \n Can you charge a power station under $300 with solar? + Yes, and quickly. The BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 takes 200W and the EcoFlow RIVER 3 Plus takes 220W, which on a 290Wh battery is roughly two hours of good sun for a full refill. Jackery does not publish a maximum solar input figure for the Explorer 300. Small batteries are the one place where solar genuinely keeps up with use. \n Is it worth waiting and spending $500 instead? + If your list includes a refrigerator, a heating appliance, or more than one night of autonomy, yes — the $500 band buys roughly three times the battery and double the inverter, which is a much bigger jump than the money suggests. If your list is phones, lights, a laptop, a camera kit and a CPAP, the sub-$300 tier does that job properly and the extra money buys capacity you will not use. \n Sources\n BLUETTI — Elite 30 V2 specifications \n EcoFlow US — RIVER 3 Plus specifications \n Jackery — Explorer 300 portable power station product page \n Anker SOLIX — C300 portable power station specifications",
