   "text": "Best Power Stations Under $300 (2026) | WattRatings Best Power Stations Under $300 in 2026\n June 7, 2026 · 7 MIN READ · BY WATTRATINGS BENCH\n Disclosure · We may earn a commission when you buy through links on this page, at no extra cost to you. As an Amazon Associate, WattRatings earns from qualifying purchases. Commissions never influence scores or verdicts — see how we rate . \n What $300 Actually Buys in 2026\n The sub-$300 power station class is where most people start, and it's also where spec sheets are most misleading. Every unit in this bracket advertises roughly the same capacity — 245 to 293Wh — so buyers assume they're interchangeable. They're not. The spec that actually separates them is the inverter, and the spread there is 2x.\n Here's the current field from our spec database:\n Station Capacity AC output Surge Chemistry Weight Max solar 0–80% charge WattScore \n Bluetti EB3A 268Wh 600W 1,200W LFP 10.1 lbs 200W 45 min 81 \n EcoFlow RIVER 3 245Wh 300W 600W LFP 7.8 lbs 110W 30 min 82 \n Anker SOLIX C300 288Wh 300W 600W LFP 9.3 lbs 100W 50 min 80 \n Jackery Explorer 300 293Wh 300W 500W NMC 7.1 lbs 100W — 74 \n Four units, three meaningfully different value propositions, and one unit you should probably skip. Let's work through them.\n The Inverter Is the Differentiator\n Capacity tells you how long a unit runs. The inverter tells you what it can run at all. In this class, capacity varies by 20% across the field while AC output varies by 100% — which means the inverter, not the battery, defines what each unit is actually for.\n A 300W inverter handles laptops (65W), CPAPs (30–60W), TVs (60–120W), camera batteries, drones, lights, and fans. It cannot start most kitchen appliances, power tools, or anything with a heating element. A 600W inverter — the Bluetti EB3A's headline spec — extends the list to small blenders, many electric kettles on low settings, projectors, and some compact refrigerators whose compressor surge would trip a 600W-surge unit.\n Surge capacity matters just as much as continuous output. Motors and compressors briefly draw 2–3x their running wattage at startup. The EB3A's 1,200W surge clears compressor startups that the 500–600W surge units choke on. If \"can it run a mini fridge?\" is your question, the answer in this class is usually \"only the EB3A\" — check your specific appliance in our can-it-run database before buying.\n Runtime: What the Math Says\n Label math (\"268Wh ÷ 60W = 4.5 hours\") ignores inverter losses and duty cycling. Our model is: runtime = Wh × 0.85 ÷ (watts × duty cycle), where 0.85 accounts for typical inverter efficiency and duty cycle reflects how often the device actually draws power. Worked examples:\n Load EB3A (268Wh) C300 (288Wh) RIVER 3 (245Wh) Explorer 300 (293Wh) \n CPAP, 40W, 100% duty ~5.7 hrs ~6.1 hrs ~5.2 hrs ~6.2 hrs \n Laptop, 65W, 100% duty ~3.5 hrs ~3.8 hrs ~3.2 hrs ~3.8 hrs \n Mini fridge, 60W, 35% duty ~10.8 hrs won't start* won't start* won't start* \n LED lights, 10W, 100% duty ~22.8 hrs ~24.5 hrs ~20.8 hrs ~24.9 hrs \n *Most compact fridges surge past 600W at compressor start; only the EB3A's 1,200W surge reliably clears it. A handful of low-surge fridges will run on the others — verify yours.\n The runtime spread across these units is roughly 15% — a half hour of laptop time. Nobody should pick between these on runtime. Run your own loads through the runtime calculator and you'll see the same pattern: in this class, capacity differences are rounding errors and inverter differences are categorical.\n Unit-by-Unit Verdicts\n Bluetti EB3A — the capability pick\n 268Wh, 600W AC, 1,200W surge, LFP, 10.1 lbs, $0–300 band, WattScore 81. The EB3A's inverter is double the class standard, and its surge is double the next best. It's also the heaviest unit here at 10.1 lbs — that's the cost of the bigger inverter. 200W of solar input is the class maximum, and 0–80% in 45 minutes is solid.\n Who it's for: anyone whose load list includes a motor, compressor, or heating element — mini fridges, blenders, small kettles. If you're not sure what you'll need to run, the EB3A is the safe default because it runs the most things. See how it stacks up directly in our EB3A vs SOLIX C300 comparison .\n EcoFlow RIVER 3 — the charging-speed pick\n 245Wh, 300W AC, 600W surge, LFP, 7.8 lbs, WattScore 82. The smallest battery in the group, but the fastest charger: 0–80% in 30 minutes is the best in the class by a wide margin. For workflows where you top up between uses — a desk UPS, car camping with short driving stints, quick pre-storm charges — charge speed substitutes for capacity.\n Who it's for: buyers running electronics only (laptop, lights, phone, router) who value the fastest recharge in the class and a sub-8 lb package. If your loads never exceed 300W, the RIVER 3's smaller battery is a fair trade. The full head-to-head with the Bluetti is in our RIVER 3 vs EB3A comparison .\n Anker SOLIX C300 — the capacity-per-dollar pick\n 288Wh, 300W AC, 600W surge, LFP, 9.3 lbs, WattScore 80. The most LFP watt-hours in the class — 7% more than the EB3A, 18% more than the RIVER 3 — with middle-of-the-road everything else. 0–80% in 50 minutes is the slowest of the LFP trio but still under an hour.\n Who it's for: electronics-only users who want maximum runtime per dollar and don't need the EB3A's inverter headroom. For CPAP users counting hours per night, the C300's extra watt-hours are the spec that matters.\n Jackery Explorer 300 — the one to skip (usually)\n 293Wh, 300W AC, 500W surge, NMC, 7.1 lbs, WattScore 74. On paper it has the most capacity in the class. In practice its NMC chemistry is rated for 500–800 cycles versus 3,000+ for the three LFP units — meaning its lifetime energy throughput is roughly a quarter of the C300's despite the similar capacity. It also has the weakest surge rating in the group — chemistry is the spec that decides how long a unit lives, and NMC loses that comparison decisively.\n Who it's for: weight-critical buyers only. At 7.1 lbs it undercuts the C300 by 2.2 lbs, and if it's deeply discounted and your use is genuinely occasional (a few cycles a year), the chemistry penalty may never bite you. At equal prices, buy LFP.\n How to Choose in 60 Seconds\n List your heaviest load. Anything with a motor or heating element → the Bluetti EB3A. Full stop.\n Electronics only, recharge often? → the EcoFlow RIVER 3 for the 30-minute 0–80%.\n Electronics only, maximize runtime? → the Anker SOLIX C300 for the most LFP watt-hours.\n Counting ounces, buying on discount, rarely cycling? → that's the Explorer 300's narrow lane.\n One honest caveat about the whole class: 245–293Wh is small. It will not back up a refrigerator through an outage or run a space heater for any meaningful time. If your real goal is home backup, this bracket is the wrong tool — start at the 1kWh class and work up. But as a first power station for camping, desk backup, CPAP travel, and keeping devices alive, the sub-$300 class in 2026 is dramatically better than it was three years ago: LFP chemistry is now standard, sub-hour charging is normal, and the EB3A's 600W inverter brings genuine appliance capability to a price point that used to mean \"phone charger with a handle.\"\n FAQ\n Can any of these run a full-size refrigerator?\n No. A typical full-size fridge draws 100–200W running but surges 600–1,200W at compressor start. Even the EB3A's 1,200W surge is marginal, and at ~268Wh you'd get only a few hours of runtime regardless. For fridge backup, look at the 1kWh class minimum — see our refrigerator can-it-run page .\n Is 268Wh enough for a CPAP overnight?\n Usually, yes — with conditions. A CPAP drawing 40W runs roughly 5.7 modeled hours on the EB3A, but turning off the heated humidifier and heated tube typically cuts draw to 10–20W, stretching runtime to 11–23 hours. Run your machine's actual wattage through our runtime calculator before relying on it.\n Why does the EB3A score 81 while the bigger Explorer 300 scores 74?\n WattScore weights inverter capability, chemistry, and charge speed alongside capacity. The EB3A's 600W/1,200W inverter and 3,000+ cycle LFP pack beat the Explorer 300's 300W/500W inverter and 500–800 cycle NMC pack by more than the Explorer's 25Wh capacity edge recovers.\n Should I just spend more for a 1,000Wh unit instead?\n If you can articulate a load that needs it — fridge backup, power tools, induction cooking — yes, the per-watt-hour economics improve at 1kWh. If your loads are phones, laptops, lights, and a CPAP, the sub-$300 class covers you and the extra 20+ lbs of a 1kWh unit is pure dead weight.\n Stay Charged\n Benchmark updates and price drops on stations we actually rate.\n Keep reading\n Oct 8, 2026 · 8 min \n Anker SOLIX C1000 vs F2000: Which Should You Buy in 2026?\n For most buyers the Anker SOLIX C1000 is the pick in 2026: 1,056Wh and 1,800W at 28.44 lb, with an 80% recharge in 43 minutes. The F2000 offers 2,048Wh, 2,400W and a TT-30R, but Anker's US store now redirects its page to the C2000 Gen 2.\n Oct 8, 2026 · 8 min \n Anker SOLIX C300 vs Jackery Explorer 300: Which Should You Buy in 2026?\n The Anker SOLIX C300 is the stronger pick in 2026 for its 50-minute recharge to 80%, 140W two-way USB-C, 10ms UPS and 5-year warranty. The Jackery Explorer 300 is lighter at 7.1 lbs and Jackery sells it direct for $289.00.\n Oct 8, 2026 · 8 min \n Can a Portable Power Station Run a Coffee Maker? (2026 Wattage Guide)\n Yes, when the station's continuous output is higher than the coffee maker's rated wattage. 300W stations cannot; the Anker SOLIX C1000 and Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 can run the 650 to 1475 W drip brewers covered here.",
