 light LiFePO4 battery for phones, laptops, lights and a CPAP. Its 300W ceiling and slow wall charging rule out appliances.\n Amazon $199 Dabbsson $199 \n $199 \n 298 Wh 300 W 9.4 lb $0.67 \n Compare Dabbsson 300E \n BLUETTI Elite 10 Mini #15 of 25 3.5 Full review \n For anyone who wants a pocket-sized battery for phones, laptops, a camera kit or a Wi-Fi router during a short outage.\n Amazon $119 BLUETTI $119 \n $119 \n 128 Wh 200 W 4 lb $0.93 \n Compare BLUETTI Elite 10 Mini \n VTOMAN FlashSpeed 300 #16 of 25 3.4 Full review \n A small, inexpensive LiFePO4 box for phones, laptops, CPAP and camp lights. Pick it for portability, not for running appliances.\n Amazon $173.99 \n $173.99 \n 230 Wh 300 W 7.4 lb $0.76 \n Compare VTOMAN FlashSpeed 300 \n EcoFlow TRAIL 200 DC #17 of 25 3.4 Full review \n A DC-only battery for charging phones, laptops and USB lights on day trips. Choose it over a large power bank when you want LiFePO4 longevity and 140W USB-C.\n Amazon EcoFlow $119 \n $119 \n 192 Wh DC only 4 lb $0.62 \n Compare EcoFlow TRAIL 200 DC \n EnginStar R300 #18 of 25 3.3 Full review \n A cheap, light 296Wh station for phones, cameras, CPAP and small camping loads. Buyers who will cycle it often should pay a little more for a LiFePO4 model with a longer warranty.\n Amazon $151.99 \n $151.99 \n 296 Wh 300 W 6.5 lb $0.51 \n Compare EnginStar R300 \n MARBERO M823 #19 of 25 3.3 Full review \n A very light, low-cost 237Wh station for charging phones, cameras and a CPAP on short trips. Buyers who need longer battery life or laptop-speed USB-C should look at LiFePO4 models.\n Amazon $139.99 \n $139.99 \n 237 Wh 300 W 4.6 lb $0.59 \n Compare MARBERO M823 \n UGREEN 300W 48000mAh Portable Power Station #20 of 25 3.2 Full review \n A large USB-C battery for photographers, travelers and campers who only need to charge laptops, phones and cameras. Skip it if anything you own needs a wall plug.\n Amazon $139.99 \n $139.99 \n 153.6 Wh DC only 3.6 lb $0.91 \n Compare UGREEN 300W 48000mAh Portable Power Station \n EcoFlow TRAIL Plus 300 DC #21 of 25 3.2 Full review \n A light DC-only station for campers who want a built-in light and app control. Choose the TRAIL 300 DC instead if long battery life matters more than weight.\n Amazon $199 \n $199 \n 288 Wh DC only 5 lb $0.69 \n Compare EcoFlow TRAIL Plus 300 DC \n Anker 521 PowerHouse #22 of 25 3.1 Full review \n For campers and travelers who want a small, long-lived LiFePO4 battery for phones, laptops, lights and CPAP. Larger or faster-charging stations give more for the money if you need to run appliances.\n Amazon $259.99 Anker SOLIX $259.99 \n $259.99 \n 256 Wh 300 W 8.2 lb $1.02 \n Compare Anker 521 PowerHouse \n Westinghouse iGen400s #23 of 25 3.0 Full review \n A cheap, very light backup for phones, laptops and small lights on a road trip or in an outage. Skip it if you need to run anything with a motor or over 200W.\n Amazon $139 \n $139 \n 183.6 Wh 200 W 5.5 lb $0.76 \n Compare Westinghouse iGen400s \n HOWEASY H120 #24 of 25 2.7 Full review \n A pocket-sized backup for phones, lights and small gadgets on a camping trip or during a short outage. Anyone who needs to run a laptop for long, a CPAP or kitchen appliances should look at a bigger station.\n Amazon $79.99 \n $79.99 \n 88 Wh 120 W 2.3 lb $0.91 \n Compare HOWEASY H120 \n Duracell M250 #25 of 25 2.6 Full review \n For people who want a tidy desk or bedside battery for phones and laptops, with wireless charging and a small AC outlet. Anyone who needs to run appliances should buy a conventional power station.\n Amazon $249 \n $249 \n 219 Wh 100 W 4.5 lb $1.14 \n Compare Duracell M250 \n Best Power Stations Under $300: Mid-size (500-1,499 Wh) \n Product Price Capacity AC output Weight $/Wh Compare \n Dabbsson 600L #1 of 4 4.0 Full review \n A low-cost, light LiFePO4 station for weekend camping and short outages that tested as efficient as far pricier rivals. Skip it if you need more than two AC outlets or fast solar charging.\n Amazon $299 Dabbsson $299 \n $299 \n 768 Wh 600 W 17.6 lb $0.39 \n Compare Dabbsson 600L \n LIBRIDS C600 #2 of 4 3.8 Full review \n A low-cost mid-size LiFePO4 station for CPAP, router and laptop backup, or for powering a campsite. It’s limited to devices under 600W and hasn’t been independently tested.\n Amazon $284.99 \n $284.99 \n 640 Wh 600 W 16.3 lb $0.45 \n Compare LIBRIDS C600 \n OUPES Exodus 700 #3 of 4 3.7 Full review \n A compact mid-size LiFePO4 station for camping and short outages, with a long warranty and a useful 140W USB-C port.\n Amazon $294.49 \n $294.49 \n 576 Wh 700 W 13.9 lb $0.51 \n Compare OUPES Exodus 700 \n DJI Power 500 #4 of 4 3.7 Full review \n A compact, quiet 512 Wh LFP station that suits DJI drone and camera users who mostly charge from the wall; buyers who want built-in solar input or a 12V socket should look elsewhere.\n Amazon $599 DJI $299 \n $299 \n 512 Wh 1,000 W 16.1 lb $0.58 \n Compare DJI Power 500 \n We'd like to use analytics cookies to see which rankings people find useful.\n They're off unless you turn them on, and we don't run ads or sell data.\n About this site \n Reject \n Accept",
