   "text": "Best Portable Power Station for Camping 2026: Tested & Ranked ✕ Home ⭐ Best Picks 📚 Buying Guides ⚖️ Comparisons 🔍 Reviews \n Best Portable Power Station for Camping 2026: Tested & Ranked\n By Editorial Team · Updated September 11, 2026\n Affiliate disclosure: We independently research and test every product. When you buy through links on this page, we may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. Learn more .\n Quick answer\n The Jackery Explorer 300 Plus is the best portable power station for camping — 288Wh of LiFePO4\nat just 8.3 lb, enough to run phones, lights, a camera, and a CPAP for multiple nights on one charge.\nThe EcoFlow River 3 Plus matches that capacity with a 600W inverter (double the Jackery’s) for a\nfew dollars less, and the Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 is the step-up pick for van life or running a\n12V fridge. For camping specifically, weight and simplicity matter more than raw watt-hours — the\n250-500Wh class is the sweet spot for most trips.\n For camping, the best power station isn’t the biggest one — it’s the one light enough to actually pack\nthat still covers your phones, lights, camera batteries, and a CPAP for the trip. The 250-500Wh class\nhits that balance, and modern LiFePO4 cells mean these little units survive years of hot, cold,\ndusty weekends. Here are the five we’d throw in the car, plus one bigger pick for van and overland\nsetups.\n Our top picks at a glance\n Model Best for Capacity Weight AC output Price Rating \n Jackery Explorer 300 Plus Best overall camping 288Wh ~8.3 lb 300W ~$249 ★★★★★ \n EcoFlow River 3 Plus Best value 286Wh ~9.9 lb 600W ~$269-299 ★★★★½ \n Anker SOLIX C300 Best for long trips 288Wh ~9.3 lb 300W (600W surge) ~$199 ★★★★½ \n Bluetti EB3A Best budget 268Wh ~10.1 lb 600W ~$179 ★★★★☆ \n Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 Best for van / overland 1,070Wh ~23.8 lb 1,500W ~$799 ★★★★½ \n Camping power by the numbers\n About 10Wh per phone charge, 30-60Wh per CPAP night, 5Wh per camp lantern night: the small draws that a 288Wh station covers for days.\n 288Wh: the capacity sweet spot — big enough for a weekend of devices, small enough to carry one-handed.\n Turn the CPAP humidifier OFF: it can triple the machine’s draw. Off, a 288Wh unit runs most CPAPs 3-5 nights.\n ~8-10 lb: the weight of a 300Wh-class station — light enough that the extra capacity is worth it over a bulky power bank.\n 1. Jackery Explorer 300 Plus — Best Overall for Camping\n Jackery Explorer 300 Plus (288Wh)\n Best overall camping · ~$249 · 288Wh LiFePO4 · 300W AC\n Just 8.3 lb with a proper carry handle — the easiest of the group to pack and move around camp.\n LiFePO4 cells and a clear, simple display; pairs plug-and-play with a Jackery SolarSaga panel.\n Runs phones, a fan, lights, and a CPAP for multiple nights on one charge.\n The Explorer 300 Plus is the best all-round camping station because it gets the basics exactly right:\nlight, simple, LiFePO4, and easy to recharge from the sun. It’s not the cheapest, but the low weight\nand Jackery’s foolproof interface make it the one you’ll actually enjoy using. For a weekend of tent or\ncar camping it covers everything short of a fridge.\n Editor’s note: Jackery’s own U.S. site no longer lists the Explorer 300 Plus among its current\nproducts — it’s been replaced by the Explorer 300 v2, which recharges faster but carries a shorter\nwarranty. The Plus remains in active stock through Amazon and other retailers. See our full Jackery\nExplorer 300 Plus review for the complete generation\ncomparison before you buy.\n 2. EcoFlow River 3 Plus — Best Value\n EcoFlow River 3 Plus (286Wh)\n Best value · ~$269-299 · 286Wh LiFePO4 · 600W AC\n A full 600W inverter (1,200W X-Boost) — double the Jackery's, so it runs more demanding gear.\n Expandable to 572Wh with a bolt-on battery for longer trips.\n Fast recharge and a slick app for the money.\n The River 3 Plus matches the Jackery on capacity while doubling the AC output and adding\nexpandability, all for a couple of dollars less. It’s slightly heavier and its interface is a touch\nbusier, but on value it’s hard to beat — the best pick if you want the most capability per dollar in\nthe compact class. Read our full EcoFlow River 3 Plus review \nfor the complete breakdown. If 286Wh is still more than you need, the smaller EcoFlow River\n3 drops to 245Wh and 300W for around $70-80 less — just don’t mix\nit up with the older, similarly-specced EcoFlow River 2 , which\ndespite its name predates the River 3 line.\n 3. Anker SOLIX C300 — Best for Long Trips\n Anker SOLIX C300 (288Wh)\n Best for long trips · ~$199 · 288Wh LiFePO4 · 300W (600W surge)\n A clever retractable LED lantern and dual USB-C ports make it a genuine multi-day camp companion.\n Fast USB-C recharge and Anker's 5-year warranty.\n Often the cheapest 288Wh LiFePO4 unit you can buy.\n The C300’s built-in lantern and strong port selection make it the most trip-friendly of the small\nunits, and Anker’s 5-year warranty is the longest here. It’s the pick for longer stretches off-grid\nwhere the integrated light and reliable USB-C fast charging earn their keep. See our full Anker\nSOLIX C300 review for why to confirm you’re buying the\nAC-outlet standard C300 and not the AC-less C300 DC variant.\n 4. Bluetti EB3A — Best Budget\n Bluetti EB3A (268Wh)\n Best budget · ~$179 · 268Wh LiFePO4 · 600W AC\n The cheapest way into a real LiFePO4 camping station, often under $180 on sale.\n 600W inverter (1,200W Power Lifting) despite the low price.\n Fast wall recharge for a budget unit.\n If you just want a dependable little power station for occasional camping and don’t want to spend much,\nthe EB3A is the value floor. It gives up some polish and its app is basic, but you get LiFePO4\nchemistry and a 600W inverter for budget-power-bank money. Only charging phones and small electronics\nand want to spend even less? Bluetti’s entry-level AC2A drops to\n204.8Wh and a 300W ceiling for about $30 less. Outgrowing the EB3A’s 268Wh on longer\ntrips? Bluetti’s own AC70 nearly triples that capacity to 768Wh\nwith a faster 1,000W inverter, for a mid-size step up before jumping to a full 1kWh station. Want a\nwider comparison of every real-LiFePO4 unit under $300, not just this one? See our\n best budget portable power station picks. Want Jackery’s\nown ecosystem instead of Bluetti’s but still on a budget? The Explorer 240\nv2 undercuts even the EB3A on weight at 256Wh and 300W,\nthough with less cycle life and output. For a full 1kWh\nstation that still recharges fast between camping stops, Bluetti’s Elite 100\nV2 hits 80% wall recharge in about 45 minutes at just 25.3 lbs.\n 5. Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 — Best for Van / Overland\n Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 (1,070Wh)\n Best for van / overland · ~$799 · 1,070Wh LiFePO4 · 1,500W AC\n Enough capacity to run a 12V fridge, fans, and devices for a full day of van life.\n 1,500W inverter (3,000W surge) covers small appliances a camp unit can't.\n Still portable at ~23.8 lb and pairs with SolarSaga panels for a solar generator.\n When a 288Wh unit isn’t enough — a 12V fridge, longer stays, or running an appliance — step up to the\nExplorer 1000 v2. It’s our overall best power station runner-up,\nand for van and overland setups the extra capacity is transformative. Add a solar panel and it becomes a\ntrue off-grid solar generator .\n How to choose a camping power station\n Weight matters more than capacity: for tent/car camping, pick the lightest unit that covers your devices — a 288Wh station you’ll actually carry beats a 1kWh one that stays in the trunk.\n Match output to your gear: phones and lights need almost nothing; a small appliance needs a 600W+ inverter and a surge headroom check.\n Get LiFePO4: it survives temperature swings and years of trips.\n Plan for solar: a small folding panel turns a weekend unit into unlimited off-grid power.\n Van/RV? Go bigger: running a 12V fridge means 1,000Wh and up.\n Not sure which capacity tier fits your gear list? Our power station sizing guide walks through the watt-hour math device by device. Living in an RV full-time or need shore-power backup rather than a grab-and-go unit? See our dedicated best RV power station picks instead.\n The bottom line\n The Jackery Explorer 300 Plus is the best camping power station for most people — light, simple,\nand long-lived. The EcoFlow River 3 Plus is the value pick, the Anker SOLIX C300 is best for\nlong trips thanks to its lantern and warranty, the Bluetti EB3A is the budget floor, and the\n Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 is the one for van and overland life. Curious how that Jackery stacks up\nagainst a rugged rival? See our Goal Zero vs Jackery breakdown.\n Frequently Asked Questions\n What size power station is best for camping? For tent and car camping, a 250-500Wh unit is the sweet spot — it charges phones, cameras, lights, a drone, and a CPAP for a night or two while staying light enough to carry. For van life or running a 12V fridge, step up to a 1,000Wh+ station and add a solar panel.\n Can I run a CPAP machine while camping? Yes. Most CPAPs draw 30-60Wh a night with the humidifier off (turn it off to save a lot of power). A 288Wh station like the Jackery Explorer 300 Plus runs a typical CPAP for 3-5 nights on a charge, or indefinitely with a small solar panel topping it up by day.\n Should a camping power station be LiFePO4? Yes. LiFePO4 cells handle heat, cold, and repeated deep discharges far better than older lithium, and last thousands of cycles. Every pick here uses LiFePO4, so it'll survive years of trips instead of fading after a couple of seasons.",
